        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            An air purifier can grow mold, but it does not happen by accident in a dry home. It takes a damp filter: a room stuck above 60 or 70 percent humidity, a washable filter put back wet, or a water tank left to sit. Fix those and the purifier goes back to doing what it is supposed to do, which is catching spores, not raising them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If your unit smells musty, unplug it, bag the filters, clean and dry the housing, and put a hygrometer in the room before you buy anything else. The number on that hygrometer will usually tell you whether the purifier was the problem or simply the first place the problem showed up.
          </p>
